   1→# VEX Corpus: Geometry Creation
   2→
   3→> 161 examples from vex-corpus. Sources: cgwiki-vex, joy-of-vex-youtube, sidefx-vex-reference
   4→
   5→## Beginner (12 examples)
   6→
   7→### Cross Product for Normals
   8→
   9→```vex
  10→@N = cross(@P, {0,1,0});
  11→```
  12→
  13→Computes point normals using the cross product between each point's position and the up vector (0,1,0).
  14→
  15→### Vector Addition and Normal Visualization
  16→
  17→```vex
  18→vector a = chv('a');
  19→vector b = chv('b');
  20→@N = a+b;
  21→```
  22→
  23→Demonstrates vector addition by reading two vector parameters from the UI and assigning their sum to the point normal attribute.
  24→
  25→### Point-Based Normal Direction
  26→
  27→```vex
  28→vector a = @P;
  29→vector b = point(1, 'P', 0);
  30→
  31→@N = b - a;
  32→```
  33→
  34→Calculates normals for all points on a sphere by creating vectors that point from each point's position (@P) toward a single reference point (point 0 from input 1).
  35→
  36→### Vector from Point to Target
  37→
  38→```vex
  39→vector p = v@P;
  40→vector b = point(1, "P", 0);
  41→
  42→v@l = b - p;
  43→
  44→vector origin = point(1, "P", 0);
  45→@v = (p - origin);
  46→```
  47→
  48→Creates vectors pointing from each point in the geometry to a single target point on the second input.
  49→
  50→### Vector Direction from Point
  51→
  52→```vex
  53→vector origin = point(1, "P", 0);
  54→@v = @P - origin;
  55→```
  56→
  57→Creates a velocity vector on each point that points away from a specific origin point (point 0 on input 1).
  58→
  59→### Coloring with relpointbbox
  60→
  61→```vex
  62→vector bbox = relpointbbox(0, @P);
  63→@Cd = bbox.y;
  64→```
  65→
  66→Uses relpointbbox() to get the relative position of each point within the bounding box of the input geometry (normalized 0-1 coordinates), then assigns the Y component of that normalized position t....
  67→
  68→### Normalizing Points and Relative Bounding Box
  69→
  70→```vex
  71→@Cd = relpointbbox(0, @P);
  72→
  73→@P = normalize(@P);
  74→
  75→vector bbox = relpointbbox(0, @P);
  76→@Cd = bbox.y;
  77→```
  78→
  79→Demonstrates using relpointbbox() to color geometry based on relative position within the bounding box.
  80→
  81→### Setting Up Vector for Orientation
  82→
  83→```vex
  84→v@up = {0,0,1};
  85→
  86→v@up = {0,1,0};
  87→
  88→v@up = {0,v.y,1};
  89→```
  90→
  91→Demonstrates setting the @up vector attribute to control orientation around the normal vector.
  92→
  93→### Declaring Vector UV Variable
  94→
  95→```vex
  96→vector uv;
  97→```
  98→
  99→Declares a vector variable named 'uv' that will be used to store UV coordinates or parametric values for sampling normals and positions from a grid geometry.
 100→
 101→### Vector Subtraction for Direction Vectors
 102→
 103→```vex
 104→vector a = {0,1,0};
 105→vector b = point(1,"P",0);
 106→
 107→@v = b-a;
 108→
 109→vector origin = point(1,"P",0);
 110→@v = @P - origin;
 111→```
 112→
 113→Demonstrates using vector subtraction to create direction vectors between points.
 114→
 115→### Setting Up Vector for Copy to Points
 116→
 117→```vex
 118→v@up = {0,0,1};
 119→
 120→v@up = {0,1,0};
 121→```
 122→
 123→Demonstrates setting the @up vector attribute to control the rotational orientation of copied geometry.
 124→
 125→### Setting Up Vector for Orientation
 126→
 127→```vex
 128→v@up = {0,0,1};
 129→
 130→v@up = {0,v.y};
 131→
 132→v@up = {0,v.y,1};
 133→```
 134→
 135→Explicitly sets the @up vector attribute to control orientation of geometry.
 136→
 137→## Intermediate (128 examples)
 138→
 139→### Combine quaternions with qmultiply â
 140→
 141→```vex
 142→vector4 foo = quaternion({1,0,0}*0.2));
 143→```
 144→
 145→Download scene: Download file: orient_qmultiply_compose.hip
 146→
 147→A trick I learned from Raph Gadot, cheers!
 148→
 149→Somewhere in the above examples are 2 quaternion tricks, using qmultiply for things, and cre....
 150→
 151→### Spiral along curve â
 152→
 153→```vex
 154→float speed, angle, rand;
 155→vector dir;
 156→vector4 q;
 157→
 158→dir = @N;
 159→speed = @Time * ch('speed');
 160→angle = speed+@curveu*ch('spirals');
 161→q = quaternion(v@t*angle);
 162→// ...
 163→```
 164→
 165→Download hip: Download file: spiral_along_curve.hip
 166→
 167→A hint from clever person HowieM led to this pleasingly clean setup.
 168→
 169→### Random dot patterns â
 170→
 171→```vex
 172→int tiles = chi('tiles');
 173→float rand = ch('rand');
 174→float start = ch('dotradius_start');
 175→float end = ch('dotradius_end');
 176→vector offset = rand(floor(@uv*tiles)) * {1,1,0};
 177→offset = fit(offset,0,1,-rand,rand);
 178→offset.z = 0;
 179→@Cd = smooth(start, end , length(frac(@uv*tiles)*{2,2,0}-{1,1,0}+offset));
 180→```
 181→
 182→The end goal.
 183→
 184→### Joy of Vex Day 3 â
 185→
 186→```vex
 187→float d = length(@P);
 188→ d *= ch('v_scale');
 189→ d += @Time;
 190→ @P.y = sin(d);
 191→```
 192→
 193→Clamp and fit, waves
 194→
 195→Hopefully you worked out those exercises from yesterday! So to make waves, we set @P.y:
 196→
 197→(If you don't view with wireframes enabled, it'll look unshaded.
 198→
 199→### Nearpoint and point â
 200→
 201→```vex
 202→int pt = nearpoint(1,@P);
 203→ @Cd = point(1,'Cd',pt);
 204→```
 205→
 206→That gif of course leads to the question.
 207→
 208→### An aside on the point function â
 209→
 210→```vex
 211→point(1, 'Cd', pt);
 212→```
 213→
 214→You might be wondering why the point function is formatted as
 215→
 216→rather than
 217→
 218→or even
 219→
 220→The last can be explained easily, @Cd without quotes would be expanded to our current point's colour.
 221→
 222→### Visualise the voronoi cell distances â
 223→
 224→```vex
 225→int pt = nearpoint(1,@P);
 226→ @Cd = point(1,'Cd',pt);
 227→ vector pos = point(1,'P',pt);
 228→ float d = distance(@P, pos);
 229→ @P.y = -d;
 230→```
 231→
 232→Back to coding!
 233→
 234→Regarding how the voronoi cells work, we can visualise the distance to the nearest scatter point by putting it into @P.y; the cell edges should end up being the same height:.
 235→
 236→### Distance-based color patterns
 237→
 238→```vex
 239→float d = distance(@P, {1,0,3});
 240→d *= ch('scale');
 241→@Cd = (sin(d)+1)*0.5;
 242→
 243→float d = length(@P) * ch('scale');
 244→@Cd = (sin(d)+1)*0.5;
 245→
 246→@Cd = (sin(length(@P) * ch('scale'))+1)*0.5;
 247→// ...
 248→```
 249→
 250→Demonstrates creating concentric ring color patterns using the distance() function to measure distance from points to a specific position in space.
 251→
 252→### Remapping sine wave with fit
 253→
 254→```vex
 255→vector pos = @P * chv('fancyscale');
 256→vector center = chv('center');
 257→float d = distance(pos, center);
 258→d *= ch('scale');
 259→@Cd = (sin(d)-.1)*.5;
 260→
 261→vector pos = @P * chv('fancyscale');
 262→vector center = chv('center');
 263→// ...
 264→```
 265→
 266→Demonstrates two approaches to remapping sine values for color: manual math adjustment using (sin(d)-.1)*.5, and the cleaner fit() function to remap sine's -1 to 1 range into 0 to 1 for valid color....
 267→
 268→### Animating with @Time
 269→
 270→```vex
 271→vector pos = @P * chv('fancyscale');
 272→vector center = chv('center');
 273→float d = distance(pos, center);
 274→d *= ch('scale');
 275→@Cd = fit(sin(d),-1,1,0,1);
 276→
 277→// Adding @Time for animation
 278→vector pos = @P * chv('fancyscale');
 279→// ...
 280→```
 281→
 282→Demonstrates animating color patterns by adding the built-in @Time attribute to the sine wave calculation.
 283→
 284→### Animating with @Frame vs @Time
 285→
 286→```vex
 287→vector pos = @P * chv('fancyscale');
 288→vector center = chv('center');
 289→float d = distance(pos, center);
 290→@Cd = fit(sin(d*@Frame),-1,1,0,1);
 291→```
 292→
 293→Demonstrates animating color patterns using @Frame for rapid integer-based animation versus @Time for smoother float-based animation.
 294→
 295→### Using fit() with channels
 296→
 297→```vex
 298→float imin = ch("fit_in_min");
 299→float imax = ch("fit_in_max");
 300→float outmin = ch("fit_out_min");
 301→float outmax = ch("fit_out_max");
 302→d = fit(d, imin, imax, outmin, outmax);
 303→@P.y = d;
 304→```
 305→
 306→Demonstrates using the fit() function to remap a variable's value range using channel references for interactive control.
 307→
 308→### Cross Product with Normalized Position
 309→
 310→```vex
 311→vector pos = point(1, "P", 0);
 312→pos = normalize(pos);
 313→@Cd = dot(@N, pos);
 314→
 315→@N = cross(@N, {0,1,0});
 316→```
 317→
 318→Reads a position from point 0 of input 1, normalizes it to create a direction vector, then computes a dot product with the surface normal to drive color.
 319→
 320→### Cross Product for Normal Direction
 321→
 322→```vex
 323→@N = cross(@N, {1,0,0});
 324→```
 325→
 326→Demonstrates using the cross product to rotate normals by crossing them with a reference axis vector.
 327→
 328→### Point-to-Point Vector Targeting
 329→
 330→```vex
 331→vector a = @P;
 332→vector b = point(1, 'P', 0);
 333→
 334→@N = b - a;
 335→
 336→vector origin = point(1, 'P', 0);
 337→@v = (@P - origin);
 338→```
 339→
 340→Creates vectors from all points in the geometry pointing toward a single reference point from the second input.
 341→
 342→### Setting velocity from origin point
 343→
 344→```vex
 345→vector origin = point(1, 'P', 0);
 346→@v = @P - origin;
 347→```
 348→
 349→Calculates velocity vectors by subtracting an origin point position from each point's position.
 350→
 351→### Setting velocity from origin point
 352→
 353→```vex
 354→vector origin = point(1, "P", 0);
 355→@v = origin;
 356→```
 357→
 358→Reads the position of point 0 from input 1 and assigns it to the velocity attribute.
 359→
 360→### Explosion Velocity from Origin Point
 361→
 362→```vex
 363→vector origin = point(1, 'P', 0);
 364→@v = (@P - origin) * ch('scale');
 365→```
 366→
 367→Creates an explosion effect by reading an origin point from the second input (input 1), computing the direction vector from that origin to each point, and scaling the result to set velocity.
 368→
 369→### Velocity From Origin Point
 370→
 371→```vex
 372→f@z = ch('scale');
 373→
 374→vector origin = point(1, 'P', 0);
 375→@v = @P - origin;
 376→```
 377→
 378→Creates velocity vectors for an explosion effect by calculating the direction from an origin point (from second input) to each point's position.
 379→
 380→### Velocity from Origin Point
 381→
 382→```vex
 383→vector origin = point(1, 'P', 0);
 384→@v = (@P - origin);
 385→
 386→@N = @v * ch('scale');
 387→```
 388→
 389→Sets velocity (@v) for each point by calculating a vector from an origin point (first point from input 1) to the current point position.
 390→
 391→### Normalizing Points and relpointbbox
 392→
 393→```vex
 394→@P = normalize(@P);
 395→
 396→@Cd = relpointbbox(0, @P);
 397→
 398→vector bbox = relpointbbox(0, @P);
 399→@Cd = bbox.y;
 400→```
 401→
 402→Demonstrates normalizing point positions to create a sphere, then using relpointbbox() to convert positions into normalized bounding box coordinates (0-1 range) for color assignment.
 403→
 404→### Relative Bounding Box Positioning
 405→
 406→```vex
 407→@P = normalize(@P);
 408→
 409→@Cd = relpointbbox(0, @P);
 410→
 411→vector bbox = relpointbbox(0, @P);
 412→@Cd = bbox.y;
 413→
 414→s@v = chv('scalevec');
 415→```
 416→
 417→Demonstrates using relpointbbox() to get a point's relative position within the bounding box of geometry.
 418→
 419→### Wave propagation from nearest point
 420→
 421→```vex
 422→int pts1[];
 423→int pt;
 424→vector pos;
 425→float d, w;
 426→
 427→pts1 = nearpoints(1, @P, ch('radius'), chi('number_of_points'));
 428→
 429→pt = pts1[0];
 430→// ...
 431→```
 432→
 433→Finds the closest point using nearpoints, calculates distance to it, and creates animated waves based on that distance using sine and frame animation.
 434→
 435→### Nearpoints ripple effect setup
 436→
 437→```vex
 438→int pts[];
 439→int pt;
 440→vector pos;
 441→float d, w;
 442→
 443→pts = nearpoints(0, @P, ch('radius'), chi('number_of_points'));
 444→
 445→pt = pts[0];
 446→// ...
 447→```
 448→
 449→Creates a ripple effect by finding nearby points from a second geometry input, calculating the distance to the closest point, and applying a sine wave modulated by distance falloff and time.
 450→
 451→### Blending ripples with falloff
 452→
 453→```vex
 454→int pts[];
 455→int pt;
 456→vector pos;
 457→float d, w;
 458→
 459→pts = nearpoints(1, @P, ch('radius'), chi('number_of_points'));
 460→
 461→pt = pts[0];
 462→// ...
 463→```
 464→
 465→Extends the ripple effect to blend smoothly across multiple influence points by calculating a falloff weight based on distance.
 466→
 467→### Creating Points Along Normal Direction
 468→
 469→```vex
 470→addpoint(0, @P + @N * 4);
 471→```
 472→
 473→Creates a new point at a position calculated by taking the current point position and offsetting it 4 units in the direction of the point's normal vector.
 474→
 475→### Creating Lines with Noise Offset
 476→
 477→```vex
 478→vector pos = @N * noise(@Frame) * {1, 0.1, 1};
 479→int pt = addpoint(0, @P + pos);
 480→addprim(0, "polyline", @ptnum, pt);
 481→```
 482→
 483→Creates a new point offset from each original point using noise-modulated normal direction, then connects each original point to its new point with a polyline primitive.
 484→
 485→### Dynamic lines from points
 486→
 487→```vex
 488→vector pos = ch('pt') * normalize(@P - @ptnum) * {1, 0.1, 1};
 489→int pt = addpoint(0, @P + pos);
 490→addprim(0, 'polyline', @ptnum, pt);
 491→```
 492→
 493→Creates dynamic waving lines by adding a new point offset from each original point using a normalized direction scaled by a channel parameter and a vector multiplier.
 494→
 495→### Creating Lines from Points
 496→
 497→```vex
 498→vector pos = ch("x") + nrandom(@ptnum) * {1,0.1,1};
 499→int pt = addpoint(0, pos);
 500→addprim(0, "polyline", @ptnum, pt);
 501→```
 502→
 503→Creates a new point offset from a channel value with random noise, then connects each original point to its corresponding new point with a polyline primitive.
 504→
 505→### Creating Polylines with Curl Noise
 506→
 507→```vex
 508→int pt = addpoint(0, @P - @N);
 509→addprim(0, "polyline", @ptnum, pt);
 510→
 511→vector pos = @N * noise(@P * @Frame) * {1, 0.1, 1};
 512→int pt = addpoint(0, @P + pos);
 513→addprim(0, "polyline", @ptnum, pt);
 514→
 515→vector offset, pos;
 516→// ...
 517→```
 518→
 519→Demonstrates creating polyline primitives procedurally using addprim(), addpoint(), and addvertex().
 520→
 521→### removeprim point retention flag
 522→
 523→```vex
 524→removeprim(0, @ptnum, 0);
 525→```
 526→
 527→The third argument of removeprim() controls whether points should be retained after primitive deletion: 1 deletes the points, 0 keeps them.
 528→
 529→### Seaweed spiral growth animation
 530→
 531→```vex
 532→if(rand(@primnum, ch('seed')) < ch('cutoff')){
 533→    removepoint(0, @primnum, 1);
 534→}
 535→
 536→// spirally scanned using sin and cos
 537→vector offset, pos;
 538→int pr, pt;
 539→int ptmax;
 540→// ...
 541→```
 542→
 543→Creates animated spiraling seaweed tendrils by generating polylines that grow from each point, using sine and cosine functions to create spiral offsets that vary with time and point number.
 544→
 545→### Color ramp with vector casting
 546→
 547→```vex
 548→float min, max, d, t;
 549→min = ch('min');
 550→max = ch('max');
 551→t = @prim * ch('speed');
 552→d = length(@P);
 553→d = d + t;
 554→s = fit(sin(d), -1, 1, min, max);
 555→@scale = set(s, s, s);
 556→// ...
 557→```
 558→
 559→Demonstrates using vector casting with chramp() to apply a color ramp to geometry.
 560→
 561→### Setting Up Vector Attribute
 562→
 563→```vex
 564→v@up = {0,0,1};
 565→```
 566→
 567→Creates an 'up' vector attribute pointing in the positive Z direction ({0,0,1}).
 568→
 569→### Workflow: Commenting and Resetting
 570→
 571→```vex
 572→vector axis;
 573→axis = chv("axis");
 574→axis = normalize(axis);
 575→@a = noise(@P*time);
 576→@a = chramp("noise_rrange",@a);
 577→axis *= trunc(@a*4)*(PI/2);
 578→@P.y = @a;
 579→@orient = quaternion(axis);
 580→// ...
 581→```
 582→
 583→Demonstrates VEX workflow techniques including commenting out lines with Ctrl+/ while preserving end-of-line comments, and resetting spare parameters between examples.
 584→
 585→### Quaternion from Matrix
 586→
 587→```vex
 588→@N = {0,1,0};
 589→float s = sin(@Time);
 590→float c = cos(@Time);
 591→@up = set(s,0,c);
 592→
 593→@orient = quaternion(maketransform(@N, @up));
 594→
 595→matrix3 m = ident();
 596→// ...
 597→```
 598→
 599→Demonstrates converting a matrix to a quaternion orientation using the quaternion() function.
 600→
 601→### Quaternion slerp animation with ramps
 602→
 603→```vex
 604→vector4 a = {0,0,0,1};
 605→vector4 b = quaternion(0,1,0,$PI/2);
 606→float blend = chramp('blendRamp',@Time%1);
 607→@orient = slerp(a, b, blend);
 608→
 609→vector4 target, base;
 610→vector axis;
 611→float seed, blend;
 612→// ...
 613→```
 614→
 615→Demonstrates quaternion interpolation using slerp() to animate orientation between base and target rotations.
 616→
 617→### Intrinsics Overview
 618→
 619→```vex
 620→v@alpha, @up, @pscale, @Ac
 621→```
 622→
 623→Introduction to intrinsic attributes in Houdini, setting up a clean scene with a sphere and transform node to begin exploring geometry intrinsics.
 624→
 625→### Declaring UV vector parameter
 626→
 627→```vex
 628→vector uv = chv("uv");
 629→```
 630→
 631→Declares a vector variable 'uv' and populates it using the chv() function to read a vector channel parameter named 'uv'.
 632→
 633→### Animating Point on Surface with primuv
 634→
 635→```vex
 636→uv.x = sin(@Frame*10);
 637→uv.y = cos(@Frame*10);
 638→
 639→UV = fit(uv, -1, 1, 0.2, 0.2);
 640→UV += {0.2, 0.5};
 641→
 642→@P = primuv(1, 'P', v.uv);
 643→@N = primuv(1, 'N', v.uv);
 644→```
 645→
 646→Demonstrates attaching a point to an animated surface position using primuv().
 647→
 648→### xyzdist on Primitive Surfaces
 649→
 650→```vex
 651→i@grid;
 652→v@uv;
 653→f@dist;
 654→
 655→@dist = xyzdist(1, @P, @primid, @uv);
 656→```
 657→
 658→Demonstrates using xyzdist() on non-polygonal primitive surfaces like tubes, spheres, and discs to compute UV coordinates.
 659→
 660→### xyzdist function setup
 661→
 662→```vex
 663→i@primid;
 664→v@uv;
 665→f@dist;
 666→
 667→@dist = xyzdist(1, @P, @primid, @uv);
 668→```
 669→
 670→Setting up the xyzdist() function to measure distance from each point to the closest primitive on input 1 (a grid).
 671→
 672→### xyzdist and primuv combined lookup
 673→
 674→```vex
 675→int @primid;
 676→vector @uv;
 677→float @dist;
 678→
 679→@dist = xyzdist(1, @P, @primid, @uv);
 680→
 681→@P = primuv(1, "P", @primid, @uv);
 682→```
 683→
 684→Uses xyzdist() to find the closest point on a surface (input 1), which returns the distance, primitive ID, and UV coordinates.
 685→
 686→### Near Points Geometry Creation
 687→
 688→```vex
 689→int pts[] = nearpoints(1,@P,ch('d'),25);
 690→int pt;
 691→vector pos;
 692→foreach (pt; pts) {
 693→    pos = point(1,"P",pt);
 694→    addpoint(0,pos);
 695→}
 696→```
 697→
 698→Finds up to 25 nearby points within a channel-controlled distance from the current point's position, then creates new geometry points at each found point's location by reading their positions and a....
 699→
 700→### Finding and Creating Points from Neighbors
 701→
 702→```vex
 703→int ps[];
 704→nearpoints(1, @P, ch('d'), 25);
 705→int pt;
 706→vector pos;
 707→foreach (pt; ps) {
 708→    pos = point(1, "P", pt);
 709→    addpoint(0, pos);
 710→}
 711→```
 712→
 713→This code finds nearby points within a radius using nearpoints(), then iterates through each found point to retrieve its position from the second input and creates new points at those positions on ....
 714→
 715→### Point Cloud Neighbor Query with Creation
 716→
 717→```vex
 718→int pts[] = nearpoints(1, @P, ch('d'), 25);
 719→int pt;
 720→vector pos;
 721→foreach (pt; pts) {
 722→    pos = point(1, "P", pt);
 723→    addpoint(0, pos);
 724→}
 725→```
 726→
 727→Uses nearpoints() to find up to 25 points within a distance threshold from the current point, then iterates through the array to read each neighbor's position and create new points at those locatio....
 728→
 729→### Nearpoint with Channel Parameter
 730→
 731→```vex
 732→int pts[] = nearpoints(1, @P, ch('d'), 25);
 733→int pt;
 734→vector pos;
 735→
 736→foreach(pt; pts){
 737→    pos = point(1, 'P', pt);
 738→    addpoint(0, pos);
 739→}
 740→```
 741→
 742→Uses nearpoints() with a channel parameter to dynamically query points within a variable distance from the current point's position.
 743→
 744→### Creating Points from Nearpoints Array
 745→
 746→```vex
 747→int pts[] = nearpoints(1, @P, ch('d'), 25);
 748→i[]@a = pts;
 749→int pt;
 750→vector pos;
 751→
 752→foreach(pt; pts){
 753→    pos = point(1, 'P', pt);
 754→    addpoint(0, pos);
 755→// ...
 756→```
 757→
 758→This code finds nearby points using nearpoints(), stores them in an array attribute, then iterates through the array to create new points at each found position.
 759→
 760→### Point Cloud Query with Point Addition
 761→
